# 15. One final distinction that's very important for our research

There are three different statements:

### A. Training data says:

```text
cow → moo
```

### B. Neural classifier learns:

$$
P(\text{moo}\mid\text{cow}) \gg
P(\text{bark}\mid\text{cow})
$$

### C. A formal rule says:

$$
cow \rightarrow moo
$$

and perhaps explicitly:

$$
cow \not\rightarrow bark
$$

**A and B are machine-learning/statistical concepts. C is a logical/semantic constraint.**

Our original animal experiment mainly demonstrated **B**, with the guardrail providing something closer to **C**.

That distinction becomes very important when we later ask the much harder question:

> **Can we put a semantic constraint into the loss function so that the neural network itself is trained to respect it?**

That's where your PyTorch experiment starts becoming quite interesting from a **formal verification + AI** perspective.

