# 15. One caution about the word "prove"

This is especially important for your eventual Isabelle work.

Suppose Isabelle proves:

```text
∀ x ∈ X. P(f(x))
```

The proof is only as strong as the formal model `f` and assumptions `X`.

If we abstract a neural network incorrectly, we might prove something about the **abstraction**, not the actual implementation.

So the real research problem becomes:

> **How do we construct a sound abstraction of a learned probabilistic system that is sufficiently precise to prove useful properties?**

That is much more interesting than simply "put an LLM into Isabelle."

