                     ▼
              Neural training
                     │
              gradient descent
                     │
                     ▼
                 MODEL
                     │
             ┌───────┴────────┐
             │                │
             ▼                ▼
       semantic test     probability
             │                │
             └───────┬────────┘
                     ▼
              Formal checker
                     │
              ┌──────┴──────┐
              ↓             ↓
            PASS          COUNTEREXAMPLE
                            │
                            ↓
                     additional training
                            │
                            └───────► repeat
```

This is very close to the **CEGIS** idea we discussed earlier:

> **Counterexample-Guided Inductive Synthesis**

The model proposes behavior; the verifier tries to break it; the counterexample becomes information for the next iteration.

---

# 22. The deepest conceptual separation

I'd keep these four statements in your mind:

| Concept          | Main question                                        |
| ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| **Learning**     | How do I acquire behavior from data?                 |
| **Optimization** | How do I change parameters to improve the objective? |
| **Probability**  | How uncertain/likely is the behavior?                |
| **Verification** | Can I prove a specified property?                    |

And **semantic constraints** sit between them:

> They tell us what kinds of behavior we actually care about.

