And **this is why I would delay the heavier mathematics** for now. You already understand enough of the conceptual machinery that, when we eventually introduce derivatives, partial derivatives, Jacobians, chain rule, probability distributions, expectations, etc., you'll know **why each mathematical object exists and where it is being used**.

The next particularly useful step would be to take our tiny `dog/cat/cow` network and make a **real PyTorch training loop of perhaps 20 lines**, then examine the actual numbers after each stage: **weights → prediction → loss → gradients → updated weights**. That would connect everything we've discussed directly to the code you're already running.


